INTEGRITY NOTE — a REAL surface bug was caught by the full build and FIXED (not hand-waved)
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The first full iOS xcodebuild (pre-fix) did NOT pass the surface: it surfaced 33 REAL errors, ALL in
  WidgetStatusGrid (33/33). Root cause = a module-level NAME COLLISION:
      WidgetStatusGrid.Adapter.swift:47: error: invalid redeclaration of 'StatusKind'
  My `enum StatusKind { ok, warning, error, inactive, unknown }` collided with a PRE-EXISTING, unrelated
  `enum StatusKind { success, warning, danger, neutral }` in TeslaSync/feature-views/helpers.Adapter.swift
  (surface "helpers"). The ambiguity cascaded into 'StatusKind is ambiguous' lookups, failed Equatable
  synthesis on StatusCell/StatusGridCell, and 'cannot infer contextual base' in the previews.
  The isolated SPM harness (separate module) could not catch this — only the real TeslaSync module did.
  FIX (in-scope, allowed files only): renamed the enum to `StatusCellKind` (surface-scoped, faithful to the
  web `StatusCell['status']`). Verified `StatusCell` / `StatusGridColumns` / `StatusGridCell` and the view
  structs are unique in the module (no other collisions). Re-ran every gate above (all green) and re-ran the
  full iOS build -> 0 WidgetStatusGrid errors. This is logged in full because the honesty covenant forbids
  red-as-green: the surface was red, was fixed, and is now green.

